states.8c8433f5612e:
[
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 08:47",
  "user": "Dev Okonjo-Reyes (Credit Analyst)",
  "text": "Kestrel Basin Foods filed an 8-K this morning, Item 4.01. Their auditor RESIGNED. Pasting the operative paragraphs below. Do we bump them from Watchlist Cat 2 to Cat 3 and ping the agent?"
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 08:48",
  "user": "Dev Okonjo-Reyes (Credit Analyst)",
  "text": "Item 4.01 Changes in Registrant's Certifying Accountant. On September 1, 2026, Kestrel Basin Foods, Inc. (the 'Company') was notified that the audit practice of Whitcombe Lowry LLP ('Whitcombe'), the Company's independent registered public accounting firm, was combined with Ostrander Hale LLP ('Ostrander') effective September 1, 2026. As a result of this transaction, on September 1, 2026, Whitcombe resigned as the Company's independent registered public accounting firm, and the Audit Committee of the Board of Directors approved the engagement of Ostrander as the Company's independent registered public accounting firm, effective the same day. The services previously provided by Whitcombe will now be provided by Ostrander.\n\nThe reports of Whitcombe on the Company's consolidated financial statements for the fiscal years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024 did not contain an adverse opinion or a disclaimer of opinion, and were not qualified or modified as to uncertainty, audit scope or accounting principles, except that the report for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025 included an explanatory paragraph regarding substantial doubt about the Company's ability to continue as a going concern.\n\nDuring the fiscal years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024 and the subsequent interim period through September 1, 2026, there were no disagreements with Whitcombe on any matter of accounting principles or practices, financial statement disclosure, or auditing scope or procedure, and there were no reportable events as defined in Item 304(a)(1)(v) of Regulation S-K. A copy of Whitcombe's letter dated September 3, 2026 stating that it agrees with the above statements is filed as Exhibit 16.1."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 08:55",
  "user": "Teodora Vance (Portfolio Manager)",
  "text": "Can someone cover the 10:30 with Hartline Packaging? I'm stuck in quarterly provisioning until noon."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 09:02",
  "user": "Marisol Brandvold (Senior Credit Officer)",
  "text": "Oh, absolutely, sound every alarm. An audit firm merges into another firm, 'resigns' from all of its clients on the same morning, and obviously that means Kestrel's books are on fire. While we're at it, let's downgrade the other sixty-odd issuers that filed the exact same 8-K this week."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 09:03",
  "user": "Marisol Brandvold (Senior Credit Officer)",
  "text": "Read past the word 'resigned'. It's a firm combination: same engagement team, new letterhead, successor engaged the same day, no disagreements, no reportable events, and the old firm signed off on all of it in the 16.1 letter. Paperwork, not a red flag."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 09:09",
  "user": "Dev Okonjo-Reyes (Credit Analyst)",
  "text": "Fair. It was the going-concern sentence in paragraph two that made me jump."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 09:14",
  "user": "Marisol Brandvold (Senior Credit Officer)",
  "text": "That's the FY2025 opinion. It was in the 10-K they filed on March 12 and it's exactly why Kestrel went to Cat 2 in April. Nothing in this 8-K changes the picture. Log it as informational in the borrower file, no category change. I'll take Hartline at 10:30."
 },
 {
  "ts": "2026-09-04 09:20",
  "user": "Teodora Vance (Portfolio Manager)",
  "text": "+1. Good instinct, Dev, just a fast read. If paragraph three had mentioned disagreements, or the auditor saying it could no longer rely on management's representations, we'd be having a very different morning. Thanks Marisol for Hartline."
 }
]

